      },
      "lineNbr": 376,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042828
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-09T21:48:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s not safe to acquire a read lock in the signal handler, at least not if the thread processing the sigal may have the write lock.\n\nPresumably there is an actual race with a write-barrier here? That seems dubious? Any affected bytes would be \"don\u0027t cares\"?\n\nIt looks like we already sometimes mark cards with byte_cas, which seems implemented strangely? Why do we distinguish architectures? But we don\u0027t read it as an atomic? Probably the card table entries should just be atomic\u003cuint8_t\u003e arrays. Otherwise we should really use atomic_ref.\n\nIf there is no real race, I would use FakeMutexLock. It\u0027s also ugly, but I think it makes things clearer and limits the damage to checking.",
      "parentUuid": "7ca26dde_3ed8e64f",
